#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""check-doc-splice - G74 comment integrity on .rs diffs: attachment on insertions (P4.18 follow-up) + deleted-definition references.

THE CLASS (2x recurred before this gate; the Loop-memory entry
`inserting-a-module-hijacks-the-preceding-doc-comment`): an edit anchored on an item's
DECLARATION line (`#[cfg(test)]` / `mod x {` / `fn y(`) inserts a NEW ITEM between a comment
block and the item that block belongs to - a `///` block genuinely re-attaches to the
insertion (proven with `#![deny(missing_docs)]`; attachment crosses BLANK lines and
ATTRIBUTE lines, so the hijack does too), and a plain `//` prose/decision-pin block (the
real P4.18 incident is a `//` block followed by a BLANK line) reads as describing the wrong
item. Both forms compile, a unified diff shows only context + adds, and no compiler lint
fires - so the catcher must read the DIFF GEOMETRY, which only exists at edit time. There is
no whole-tree form: a snapshot cannot distinguish a hijacked attachment from an authored one
(leg (a) is diff/range-scoped BY NATURE - unlike G8/G70 there is no `--full` re-cover; the
L1 staged + L2/L4 range legs are the whole coverage).

THE RULE (calibrated against the repo's full real history + rustc ground truth): the scan
tracks an ATTACHMENT state over each hunk's context/added lines - a PROSE comment line
(any `//`-family line containing a letter, or a block-comment body/tail; a letter-free
divider like `// ----` is NOT prose and never opens an attachment) sets it, BLANK lines and
ATTRIBUTE lines carry it through (rustc attaches across both), any other code line clears
it, a REMOVED line clears it (a declaration REPLACEMENT - rename / signature change /
rustfmt reflow - re-declares the same attachment owner and must not fire; the recorded
residual: the reset is content-blind, so an edit that also touches the line above the
insertion is outside the modeled pure-insertion class). An added run FAILS iff the
attachment state is set at its start AND the run DECLARES AN ITEM (an item keyword on any
run line; in an attribute-led run also the `name!(...)` item-macro form). Runs that only
extend the comment (the repo-mandated `[Build-Session-Entscheidung]`/`[Test-Change]` tag
inserts - rustc-proven harmless) or only add attributes pass. Over-strict residual: a
genuinely-new item spliced under a foreign PROSE comment always fires; the fix for a false
positive (move the insertion below the documented item's closing brace, or move a trailing
end-of-section note onto its own item's line) IS the fix for a true hijack - no escape
marker.

CONTEXT WINDOW: both modes diff with -U10 - the attachment source must be visible IN the hunk,
so a prose comment separated from the insertion by MORE than 10 unchanged carry (blank/attr)
lines is outside the window (a recorded residual; the in-tree maximum observed is 1 blank line,
and every hunk boundary honestly RESETS the state rather than guessing). `---`/`+++` are read as
file headers only BEFORE a chunk's first `@@`: an added body line whose content begins `++ `
renders as `+++ …` and must not re-key the file (2026-09-08, both legs). The input is a git unified diff:
every file section is introduced by `diff --git`, which is what resets the header state.

LEG (c) - DELETED-DEFINITION DANGLING REFERENCES (2026-09-08): a comment citing, in backticks, an
item whose definition the scanned diff removes and no tracked first-party `.rs` file still declares
(three comments cited the `*_exhaustive` helpers the `complete_kind_list!` refactor deleted; rustc
reads no comment and G73 resolves only the ipc/** `_contract` family). The deleted set exists only
in a diff, so the leg shares leg (a)'s modes; the resolver is the whole tracked tree, so a name
re-defined anywhere resolves. Only backticked references count - a plain word in prose is prose. A
whole-file deletion (`+++ /dev/null`) keys its definitions by the old path. Recorded residuals, over-strict
in leg (a)'s safe direction: a deliberate same-commit narration of a removed symbol fires, and so does a
backticked mention of the same NAME meaning something else (a third-party `classify` beside a deleted
first-party `classify`) - the remedy is to reword to the surviving mechanism or a module anchor, no escape
marker; the resolver reads the worktree while `--diff` scans the index; a tree git cannot list skips with
a notice. Both live diffs include DELETIONS and typechanges (`--diff-filter=ACMRDT` - the first cut's `ACMR` kept every
removed file out of the leg, the round-3 G1 finding; leg (a) skips a `+++ /dev/null` hunk by itself), and both
carry the diff-SYNTAX pins (`a/`/`b/` prefixes forced, blank context never elided, hunks never merged, paths never
relative, no external driver, no textconv, every file read as text, non-ASCII literal - a user's `diff.noprefix`/
`diff.mnemonicPrefix`/`diff.suppressBlankEmpty`/`diff.external` or a `binary` attribute otherwise re-shapes or
replaces the patch the scans read, the round-12/13 G1 class) and run without GIT_DIFF_OPTS (it outranks the explicit
-U10 the attachment scan needs); a git-quoted header path is C-unquoted; lines are split on git's `\n` only, from
bytes - a lone CR inside a comment forged a definition line under `str.splitlines()` (the round-14 finding). The
definition scan is the keyword forms plus the crate's own `complete_kind_list!` heads (const, test and
`elsewhere` names); an item any OTHER macro declares is invisible to it - a recorded residual. The
resolver's source scope is deliberately WIDER than plan-lint check 29's scan (`fuzz/` and `tests/` are in,
so a name defined there resolves); only the planted `tests/g53-fixture` tree is out, as it is there. The
removed-side definition scan is line-anchored on the raw `-` lines, not comment-stripped: a
definition-shaped line inside a removed `/* ... */` body is a phantom deletion (over-strict, the safe
direction; no such line exists in-tree) - a recorded residual. The resolver is NAME-based: a definition of
the same name anywhere in the tree - one this very diff adds in another file included - resolves the
reference, so a comment citing a genuinely removed `helper` stays silent while an unrelated `helper` exists
(the mirror of the over-strict same-name residual; the safe direction is not available to a name resolver).

MODES
  --diff              L1 (pre-commit): the STAGED diff (`git diff --cached -U10 -- *.rs`).
                      FAIL-OPEN with a notice when there is no HEAD to diff against.
  --range [--base S]  L2 (pre-push): every unpushed commit (`git diff <base>..HEAD`); the
                      base falls back through `@{u}` -> `origin/<branch>` -> `origin/main` ->
                      `origin/HEAD` (the run-gitleaks chain). L4 (CI): `--base` is the push
                      event's `before` SHA; an EMPTY / all-zeros / unresolvable base SKIPS
                      with a notice (pull_request / schedule / first push - the
                      check-l-neg1-ack posture; the ci.yml step quotes the expansion so an
                      absent event field arrives as an empty string, never a missing arg).

stdlib-only. This script is L(-1) security-critical (scripts/l-neg1-files.toml, G71).
Exit: 0 = clean (or fail-open/skip with notice); 1 = a hijack-shaped insertion or a comment citing a
      definition the diff removes; 2 = git error.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
for _stream in (sys.stdout, sys.stderr):          # the console's codepage is not this script's concern (G9 invariant i)
    if hasattr(_stream, "reconfigure"):
        _stream.reconfigure(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")

# An item-introducing keyword - the signal that an added run DECLARES A NEW ITEM. Optional
# visibility, then the fn/impl/trait modifier chain (default/async/unsafe/const + an
# extern-ABI prefix), then the item keyword itself; the regex BACKTRACKS so `const X:` /
# `static S:` still match as items after the modifier loop. (`macro_rules!` matches via the
# keyword alternation; the attr-led `name!(..)` item-macro form is _ITEM_MACRO's, below.)
_ITEM_KW = re.compile(
    r"^\s*(?:pub(?:\s*\([^)]*\))?\s+)?"
    r"(?:(?:default|async|unsafe|const)\s+)*"      # fn/impl/trait modifier chain (async fn, unsafe extern fn, const fn)
    r"(?:extern\s*\"[^\"]*\"\s+)?"                # an extern-ABI fn prefix (unsafe extern \"C\" fn)
    r"(?:mod|fn|struct|enum|union|trait|impl|const|static|type|macro_rules!|extern)\b"
)
# `pub use` re-exports: rustdoc attaches a /// to a re-export, so a spliced `pub use` steals
# docs like any item. Bare `use` is NOT counted (a body `use std::io;` after an explanatory
# comment is an everyday non-item edit).
_PUB_USE = re.compile(r"^\s*pub(?:\s*\([^)]*\))?\s+use\b")
# The item-macro form (`define_test_module!(x);` / `name! { ... }`) - counted as an item
# declaration ONLY in an attribute-led run (an attr in item position pins the macro as an
# item; a bare macro CALL after an explanatory comment inside a body must not fire).
_ITEM_MACRO = re.compile(r"^\s*[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*(?:::[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)*!\s*[({\[]")
_ATTR_LINE = re.compile(r"^\s*#\s*\[")
_COMMENT_LINE = re.compile(r"^\s*(//|/\*|\*)")     # any //-family line or block-comment body/opener
_HAS_LETTER = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z]")


def _line_kind(line: str) -> str:
    """Classify one context/added line for the attachment state machine:
    'comment' (a PROSE comment - it can own an attachment), 'carry' (blank or attribute -
    rustc attaches ACROSS these), or 'code' (clears any attachment)."""
    if not line.strip():
        return "carry"
    if _ATTR_LINE.match(line):
        return "carry"
    if _COMMENT_LINE.match(line) or re.search(r"\*/\s*$", line):
        # a letter-free divider (`// ----`, `//======`) is punctuation, not prose - it owns
        # no attachment a splice could steal.
        return "comment" if _HAS_LETTER.search(line) else "carry"
    return "code"


def _run_declares_item(added: list[str]) -> bool:
    """Does this added run declare a new item (vs only extending the comment block or adding
    attributes to the item the block already documents)?"""
    if any(_ITEM_KW.match(line) or _PUB_USE.match(line) for line in added):
        return True
    if _ATTR_LINE.match(added[0]):
        # an attribute-led run is in ITEM position - a macro invocation there is an item.
        return any(_ITEM_MACRO.match(line) for line in added if not _ATTR_LINE.match(line)
                   and not _COMMENT_LINE.match(line))
    return False


def _unquote(s: str) -> str:
    """(pure) Undo git's C-style path quoting: `\\"`, `\\\\`, `\\t`, `\\n`, `\\r` and `\\ooo` octal bytes (UTF-8)."""
    out = bytearray()
    i = 0
    while i < len(s):
        c = s[i]
        if c == "\\" and i + 1 < len(s):
            if i + 3 < len(s) and all(ch in "01234567" for ch in s[i + 1:i + 4]):
                out.append(int(s[i + 1:i + 4], 8))
                i += 4
                continue
            n = s[i + 1]
            out.extend({"n": b"\n", "t": b"\t", "r": b"\r"}.get(n, n.encode("utf-8")))
            i += 2
            continue
        out.extend(c.encode("utf-8"))
        i += 1
    return out.decode("utf-8", errors="replace")


def _header_path(s: str) -> str:
    """(pure) The path of a `--- `/`+++ ` header line, C-unquoted when git quoted it (a `"`, a `\\` or a control
    char in the name; the G70 refinement fix swept to this sibling - the 2026-09-08 review's round 13)."""
    s = s.strip()
    return _unquote(s[1:-1]) if len(s) >= 2 and s[0] == '"' and s[-1] == '"' else s


def scan_unified_diff(diff_text: str) -> list[str]:
    """Return one problem string per hijack-shaped added run in a unified diff (any .rs file)."""
    problems: list[str] = []
    current_file = ""
    in_rs = False
    new_lineno = 0
    attach = False                        # a prose-comment attachment is open above the cursor
    pending_run: list[str] = []
    run_start_line = 0
    run_in_attachment = False

    def flush() -> None:
        nonlocal pending_run
        if pending_run and run_in_attachment and _run_declares_item(pending_run):
            problems.append(
                f"{current_file}:{run_start_line}: an inserted ITEM lands inside the attachment of "
                f"a comment block that belongs to the NEXT item (first added line: "
                f"{pending_run[0].strip()[:80]!r}) - the block above will read as if it describes "
                "the insertion, and a `///` block genuinely re-attaches to it (attachment crosses "
                "blank and attribute lines). Move the insertion BELOW the documented item's closing "
                "brace (anchor on the end of the item, never on its declaration/attribute line); if "
                "the comment above is a trailing end-of-section note, move it onto its own item "
                "instead. See the Loop-memory entry "
                "`inserting-a-module-hijacks-the-preceding-doc-comment`."
            )
        pending_run = []

    def feed(line: str) -> None:
        """Advance the attachment state machine with one context/added line."""
        nonlocal attach
        kind = _line_kind(line)
        if kind == "comment":
            attach = True
        elif kind == "code":
            attach = False
        # 'carry': blank/attr - attachment passes through unchanged

    in_hunk = False                       # `---`/`+++` are headers only BEFORE a chunk's first `@@`
    for raw in _lines(diff_text):
        if raw.startswith("diff --git "):
            flush()
            in_hunk = False
            continue
        if not in_hunk and raw.startswith("--- "):
            continue
        if not in_hunk and raw.startswith("+++ "):
            # An ADDED body line whose content begins `++ ` renders as `+++ …` too; only the pre-hunk
            # header re-keys the file (the round-8 G1 finding: the guard leg (c) got in round 7, swept here).
            flush()
            current_file = _header_path(raw[4:])
            if current_file.startswith("b/"):
                current_file = current_file[2:]
            in_rs = current_file.endswith(".rs")
            attach = False
            continue
        if raw.startswith("@@"):
            in_hunk = True
            if not in_rs:
                continue
            flush()
            m = re.search(r"\+(\d+)", raw)
            new_lineno = int(m.group(1)) - 1 if m else 0
            attach = False                # hunk boundary: attachment context unknown
            continue
        if not in_rs:
            continue
        if raw.startswith("+"):
            new_lineno += 1
            if not pending_run:
                run_start_line = new_lineno
                run_in_attachment = attach
            pending_run.append(raw[1:])
            feed(raw[1:])
            continue
        if raw.startswith("-"):
            # a removal ends the pure-insertion shape AND clears the attachment: a declaration
            # REPLACED under its comment block (rename / signature change / rustfmt reflow)
            # re-declares the SAME owner - never a steal. Recorded residual: the reset is
            # content-blind (an edit also touching the line above the insertion falls outside
            # the modeled pure-insertion class).
            flush()
            attach = False
            continue
        if raw.startswith("\\"):
            continue                          # the `\ No newline at end of file` marker is not a line
        # context line
        flush()
        new_lineno += 1
        feed(raw[1:] if raw.startswith(" ") else raw)
    flush()
    return problems


# --- leg (c): deleted-definition dangling references (2026-09-08) -----------------------------------
# THE CLASS: a comment cites a symbol in backticks (`engine_id_exhaustive`) and the same change deletes
# that symbol's definition - rustc reads no comment, and G73 resolves only the ipc/** `_contract`
# family. Three comments cited the `*_exhaustive` helpers the `complete_kind_list!` refactor removed
# (the residual-closure review's P1). The deleted set only exists in a diff, so this leg is
# diff/range-scoped like leg (a); the RESOLVER is whole-tree - every tracked first-party `.rs` file's
# definitions, collected over a comment/string-stripped projection - so a name re-defined anywhere
# resolves. Only a BACKTICKED reference counts (`name`, `path::name`, `name()`, `name!`): a plain word
# in prose is prose. The definition forms are the item-declaring keywords leg (a) already models.
_DEF_LINE = re.compile(
    r"^\s*(?:pub(?:\s*\([^)]*\))?\s+)?"
    r"(?:(?:default|async|unsafe|const)\s+)*"
    r"(?:extern\s*\"[^\"]*\"\s+)?"
    r"(?:fn|mod|struct|enum|union|trait|type|const|static|macro_rules!)\s+([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)",
    re.ASCII | re.MULTILINE,
)
_SOURCE_PREFIXES = ("src-tauri/", "crates/", "xtask/", "fuzz/", "tests/")
_SOURCE_EXCLUDES = ("tests/g53-fixture/",)          # the planted copyleft fixture: out, as for plan-lint check 29
# The crate's one item-declaring macro: `complete_kind_list!(NAME, test_name: Ty = [...], elsewhere ENAME = [...])`
# declares NAME, test_name and ENAME. A keyword scan cannot see them, so a hand-written `const` converted to a
# macro-generated one would sit in the deleted set although it still exists (the round-3 G1 finding).
_MACRO_HEAD = re.compile(r"complete_kind_list!\s*\(\s*([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\s*,\s*([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)", re.ASCII)
_MACRO_ELSEWHERE = re.compile(r"\belsewhere\s+([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\s*=", re.ASCII)


def net_deleted_definitions(diff_text: str) -> dict[str, str]:
    """(pure) {name: file} for every item definition a `-` line of a `.rs` hunk removes that no `+` line
    anywhere in the diff re-declares (a move/rename-in-place is not a deletion)."""
    removed: dict[str, str] = {}
    added: set[str] = set()
    current = ""
    old_path = ""
    in_hunk = False                       # `---`/`+++` are headers only BEFORE a chunk's first `@@`
    for raw in _lines(diff_text):
        if raw.startswith("diff --git "):
            in_hunk = False
            continue
        if raw.startswith("@@"):
            in_hunk = True
            continue
        if not in_hunk and raw.startswith("--- "):
            old_path = _header_path(raw[4:])
            old_path = old_path[2:] if old_path.startswith("a/") else old_path
            continue
        if not in_hunk and raw.startswith("+++ "):
            current = _header_path(raw[4:])
            current = current[2:] if current.startswith("b/") else current
            if current == "/dev/null":            # a WHOLE-FILE deletion: the old path is the identity
                current = old_path
            continue
        if not current.endswith(".rs"):
            continue
        if raw.startswith("-"):
            mt = _DEF_LINE.match(raw[1:])
            if mt:
                removed.setdefault(mt.group(1), current)
        elif raw.startswith("+"):
            mt = _DEF_LINE.match(raw[1:])
            if mt:
                added.add(mt.group(1))
    return {name: file for name, file in removed.items() if name not in added}


def _comment_spans(line: str, in_block: bool) -> tuple[str, bool]:
    """The comment text of `line` given the prior block-comment state, plus the new state. STATEFUL
    (so a `/* ... */` body line with no leading `*` is still comment) and STRING-AWARE (so a `//` or
    `/*` inside a "..."/'...'/`...` literal - e.g. a URL "http://x" - is NOT a comment start). Returns
    (comment_text, in_block_after). Adapted verbatim from scripts/check-rs-test-refs (P2.136); the
    stdlib gate scripts are self-contained and do not import one another."""
    if in_block:
        end = line.find("*/")
        if end == -1:
            return line, True
        rest_c, nb = _comment_spans(line[end + 2:], False)
        return line[:end] + "\n" + rest_c, nb
    i, n = 0, len(line)
    while i < n:
        c = line[i]
        if c in "\"'`":
            j, closed = i + 1, False
            while j < n:
                if line[j] == "\\":
                    j += 2
                    continue
                if line[j] == c:
                    closed = True
                    break
                j += 1
            if closed:
                i = j + 1
                continue
            i += 1
            continue
        if c == "/" and i + 1 < n and line[i + 1] == "/":
            return line[i + 2:], False
        if c == "/" and i + 1 < n and line[i + 1] == "*":
            end = line.find("*/", i + 2)
            if end == -1:
                return line[i + 2:], True
            rest_c, nb = _comment_spans(line[end + 2:], False)
            return line[i + 2:end] + "\n" + rest_c, nb
        i += 1
    return "", False


def _strip_rust(text: str) -> str:
    """(pure) `text` with every COMMENT, STRING, CHAR and RAW-STRING body replaced by spaces (newlines
    preserved), so an item keyword inside a comment or string body is never a phantom definition. Same
    length + newline positions as the input. Adapted verbatim from scripts/check-rs-test-refs (P2.136)."""
    out: list[str] = []
    i, n = 0, len(text)
    while i < n:
        c = text[i]
        if c == "\n":
            out.append("\n"); i += 1
        elif c == "/" and i + 1 < n and text[i + 1] == "/":
            while i < n and text[i] != "\n":
                out.append(" "); i += 1
        elif c == "/" and i + 1 < n and text[i + 1] == "*":
            out.append("  "); i += 2; depth = 1
            while i < n and depth > 0:
                if text[i] == "\n":
                    out.append("\n"); i += 1
                elif text[i] == "/" and i + 1 < n and text[i + 1] == "*":
                    out.append("  "); i += 2; depth += 1
                elif text[i] == "*" and i + 1 < n and text[i + 1] == "/":
                    out.append("  "); i += 2; depth -= 1
                else:
                    out.append(" "); i += 1
        elif c == "r" and i + 1 < n and (text[i + 1] == '"' or text[i + 1] == "#"):
            k = i + 1
            while k < n and text[k] == "#":
                k += 1
            if k < n and text[k] == '"':
                hashes = k - (i + 1)
                out.append(" " * (k - i + 1)); i = k + 1
                closer = '"' + "#" * hashes
                while i < n:
                    if text[i] == "\n":
                        out.append("\n"); i += 1
                    elif text.startswith(closer, i):
                        out.append(" " * len(closer)); i += len(closer); break
                    else:
                        out.append(" "); i += 1
            else:
                out.append(c); i += 1
        elif c == '"':
            out.append(" "); i += 1
            while i < n:
                if text[i] == "\\" and i + 1 < n:
                    out.append(" \n" if text[i + 1] == "\n" else "  "); i += 2
                elif text[i] == '"':
                    out.append(" "); i += 1; break
                elif text[i] == "\n":
                    out.append("\n"); i += 1
                else:
                    out.append(" "); i += 1
        elif c == "'":
            if i + 1 < n and text[i + 1] == "\\":
                j = i + 2
                while j < n and j < i + 12 and text[j] not in ("'", "\n"):
                    j += 1
                if j < n and text[j] == "'":
                    out.append(" " * (j - i + 1)); i = j + 1
                else:
                    out.append("'"); i += 1
            elif i + 2 < n and text[i + 2] == "'":
                out.append("   "); i += 3
            else:
                out.append("'"); i += 1
        else:
            out.append(c); i += 1
    return "".join(out)


def collect_definitions(text: str) -> set[str]:
    """(pure) every item name `text` DEFINES, over the stripped projection (no phantom defs in comments):
    the keyword forms plus the names `complete_kind_list!` heads declare."""
    stripped = _strip_rust(text)
    names = {mt.group(1) for mt in _DEF_LINE.finditer(stripped)}
    for mt in _MACRO_HEAD.finditer(stripped):
        names.update(mt.groups())
    names.update(mt.group(1) for mt in _MACRO_ELSEWHERE.finditer(stripped))
    return names


def dangling_deleted_refs(deleted: dict[str, str], files: dict[str, str]) -> list[str]:
    """(pure) one problem per backticked COMMENT reference to a deleted name that no file in `files`
    still defines. `deleted` is net_deleted_definitions' {name: file}; `files` is {rel: text}."""
    if not deleted:
        return []
    defined: set[str] = set()
    for text in files.values():
        defined |= collect_definitions(text)
    gone = {name: file for name, file in deleted.items() if name not in defined}
    if not gone:
        return []
    ref_re = re.compile(
        r"`(?:[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*::)*(" + "|".join(re.escape(n) for n in sorted(gone)) + r")(?:!|\(\))?`",
        re.ASCII,
    )
    problems: list[str] = []
    for rel in sorted(files):
        in_block = False
        for lineno, line in enumerate(_lines(files[rel]), 1):
            comment, in_block = _comment_spans(line, in_block)
            for mt in ref_re.finditer(comment):
                problems.append(
                    f"{rel}:{lineno}: the comment cites `{mt.group(1)}`, whose definition this diff removes "
                    f"({gone[mt.group(1)]}) and no tracked .rs file still declares - repoint the comment at "
                    "the surviving mechanism (or a module anchor), never at a symbol that no longer exists"
                )
    return problems


def _tracked_sources() -> dict[str, str]:
    """{rel: text} for every git-tracked first-party `.rs` file (the definition set + the comment corpus),
    read from the WORKTREE under the repo root (recorded residual: `--diff` scans the index, so a partially
    staged file's unstaged comment text is what the resolver sees). Empty when git cannot list the tree -
    the caller reports that as a skip notice, never as a clean pass."""
    rc, root = _git("rev-parse", "--show-toplevel")
    if rc != 0:
        return {}
    rc, out = _git("ls-files", "-z", "--full-name", "--", ":/*.rs")
    if rc != 0:
        return {}
    files: dict[str, str] = {}
    base = root.strip()
    for rel in out.split("\0"):
        if rel and rel.startswith(_SOURCE_PREFIXES) and not rel.startswith(_SOURCE_EXCLUDES):
            try:
                with open(f"{base}/{rel}", "rb") as fh:
                    files[rel] = _decode(fh.read())
            except OSError:
                continue
    return files


def _lines(text: str) -> list[str]:
    """(pure) git's line model: split on `\\n` ONLY - a lone CR or form feed stays inside its line (under
    `str.splitlines()` it opened a new line, and a CR-joined pseudo-line forged a tag / a definition: the 2026-09-08
    review's round 14); a trailing newline ends the last line without opening an empty one."""
    parts = text.split("\n")
    if parts and parts[-1] == "":
        parts.pop()
    return parts


def _decode(raw: bytes) -> str:
    """(pure) A git child's bytes as text - decoded here, never through a text-mode pipe, so no universal-newline
    translation turns a lone CR into a line break before `_lines` sees it."""
    return raw.decode("utf-8", errors="replace")


def _git(*args: str) -> tuple[int, str]:
    p = subprocess.run(["git", *args], env=_git_env(), capture_output=True)
    return p.returncode, _decode((p.stdout or b"") + (p.stderr or b""))


# The diff SYNTAX the parsers rely on, pinned on the command line: a `-c` outranks every config file and the
# flags outrank the attributes, so the keys below cannot be re-shaped by a user's or the repo's git config (the
# 2026-09-08 residual-closure review, rounds 12-13: `diff.noprefix=true` left every `diff --git` line unseedable
# and a pair-less deletion passed SILENTLY; `diff.mnemonicPrefix=true` re-keyed a deleted file to `c/...`;
# `diff.suppressBlankEmpty=true` elided blank context lines and compressed a tag window; a `diff.external`
# driver or a textconv filter replaced the patch text or failed the read into a skip-with-notice; git's own
# binary detection - one NUL byte - or a `binary` attribute hid a modified file behind `Binary files differ`):
# non-ASCII paths literal, `a/`/`b/` prefixes forced, blank context lines never elided, hunks never merged, paths
# never relative, no external driver, no textconv, every file read as text, no colour. The one environment knob
# git honours OVER the command line, GIT_DIFF_OPTS (it overrides an explicit `--unified`), is scrubbed from every
# git read (`_git_env`). Named residual: `diff.algorithm`/`diff.indentHeuristic` choose between EQUIVALENT
# renderings of an ambiguous change - every removed line is still a `-` line and every added line a `+` line,
# so no verdict flips. One constant pair + one scrub list on every DIFF read; every NAME LIST is read `-z`
# (NUL-separated, never quoted - the round-15 finding: the fail-closed --full mirrors listed `ls-files` newline-split
# and a quoted non-ASCII name failed their extension test) - both pinned by the canary.
# Lines are split on git's `\n` ONLY and every git read is decoded from BYTES (`_lines` / `_decode`): under
# `str.splitlines()` + a text-mode pipe's universal newlines, a lone CR or form feed inside a line opened a new
# line: a CR-joined comment forged a definition line and an added-line map desynced from its blob's numbering
# (the round-14 finding); the CR byte itself is G52's to reject at L1.
GIT_DIFF_SYNTAX = ("-c", "core.quotepath=false", "-c", "diff.noprefix=false", "-c", "diff.mnemonicPrefix=false",
                   "-c", "diff.srcPrefix=a/", "-c", "diff.dstPrefix=b/", "-c", "diff.suppressBlankEmpty=false",
                   "-c", "diff.relative=false", "-c", "diff.interHunkContext=0")
GIT_DIFF_FLAGS = ("--no-ext-diff", "--no-textconv", "--text", "--no-color")
SCRUBBED_GIT_ENV = ("GIT_DIFF_OPTS", "GIT_EXTERNAL_DIFF")


def _git_env() -> dict[str, str]:
    """The git child's environment without the knobs git honours OVER the command line (SCRUBBED_GIT_ENV)."""
    return {k: v for k, v in os.environ.items() if k not in SCRUBBED_GIT_ENV}


def _staged_diff() -> tuple[int, str]:
    rc, head = _git("rev-parse", "--verify", "HEAD")
    if rc != 0:
        return 0, ""                      # unborn branch: nothing to diff against (fail-open)
    return _git(*GIT_DIFF_SYNTAX, "diff", "--cached", "-U10", *GIT_DIFF_FLAGS, "--diff-filter=ACMRDT", "--", ":/*.rs")


def _range_base(explicit: str | None) -> str | None:
    if explicit is not None:
        if not explicit or set(explicit) == {"0"}:
            return None                   # empty (pull_request/schedule) or first-push zero SHA
        rc, _ = _git("rev-parse", "--verify", f"{explicit}^{{commit}}")
        return explicit if rc == 0 else None
    cands = ["@{u}"]
    rc, branch = _git("rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD")
    if rc == 0 and branch.strip() and branch.strip() != "HEAD":
        cands.append(f"origin/{branch.strip()}")
    cands += ["origin/main", "origin/HEAD"]
    for c in cands:
        rc, _ = _git("rev-parse", "--verify", f"{c}^{{commit}}")
        if rc == 0:
            return c
    return None


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description="G74 comment-attachment integrity on .rs insertions")
    mode = ap.add_mutually_exclusive_group(required=True)
    mode.add_argument("--diff", action="store_true", help="L1: scan the staged diff (fail-open w/o base)")
    mode.add_argument("--range", action="store_true", help="L2/L4: scan <base>..HEAD")
    ap.add_argument("--base", default=None, help="explicit range base (CI: the push event's before SHA; "
                                                 "an empty value skips with a notice)")
    args = ap.parse_args(argv)

    if args.diff:
        rc, diff = _staged_diff()
        if rc != 0:
            print(f"[G74] git diff failed: {diff.strip()[:200]}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        if not diff:
            print("[G74] doc-splice: no staged .rs changes (or unborn branch) - nothing to scan")
            return 0
    else:
        base = _range_base(args.base)
        if base is None:
            print("[G74] doc-splice: no resolvable range base (pull_request/schedule/first push) - "
                  "skipped with notice; the L1 staged-diff leg covered each commit at its edit")
            return 0
        rc, diff = _git(*GIT_DIFF_SYNTAX, "diff", "-U10", *GIT_DIFF_FLAGS, "--diff-filter=ACMRDT", f"{base}..HEAD", "--", ":/*.rs")
        if rc != 0:
            print(f"[G74] git diff {base}..HEAD failed: {diff.strip()[:200]}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2

    problems = scan_unified_diff(diff)
    deleted = net_deleted_definitions(diff)
    if deleted:
        sources = _tracked_sources()
        if sources:
            problems += dangling_deleted_refs(deleted, sources)
        else:
            print("[G74] deleted-definition references: no tracked .rs source readable (git ls-files / the "
                  "worktree) - leg (c) skipped with notice")
    if problems:
        for p in problems:
            print(f"[G74] FAIL: {p}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    print("[G74] doc-splice: no comment-attachment hijack in the scanned .rs insertions and no "
          "comment citing a definition the diff removes")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
